---
/**
 * Terms of use.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3).
 *
 * TWO DISCIPLINES GOVERN THIS FILE.
 *
 * First, it states only what the product provably does. Re-verified in this session at the reviewed
 * date: packages/rights-engine/src/index.ts is `export {}` and data/rights/ holds only `sources/`,
 * so no rule set exists and no rights assessment is produced from one; .env.example:76 pins
 * FLIGHT_PROVIDER=fixture and docs/PROVIDER_LICENSING.md records no licensed provider, so live
 * flight data is Unavailable; there is no account, no purchase, and no code path that submits
 * anything to a third party on a user's behalf. The one rights card the site renders — on
 * /methodology/ — is fixture content carrying the `Demo` chip, the caption "Demo data — not a live
 * flight." and a rule set labelled `Demo rule set`, which is exactly the exception this page names.
 *
 * Second, the quoted disclaimers are verbatim fixed text — the independence disclaimer from
 * DIRECTIVE.md §3.4 and three of the §26 result disclaimers. They are rendered from constants so
 * that reformatting cannot reflow them, and they are not paraphrasable: AGENTS.md §7 requires
 * escalation rather than an edit if one reads badly.
 *
 * The liability and governing-law section is deliberately unwritten. Naming a jurisdiction the
 * operator has not designated would be an invented fact (AGENTS.md §1.1), and a limitation of
 * liability that no lawyer has read is worth less than the honesty of saying so. Human legal review
 * of this page is Blocked (external).
 *
 * THE NAME OF THIS DOCUMENT. It is "Terms of use" — in the title, in the h1, and in every sentence
 * on the five policy pages that names it. The footer calls the same route "Terms"
 * (`nav.footer.links['/terms/']`), and that stays as it is. A dense column of nav labels and a
 * sentence naming a document by its title are two registers, both correct, and this is a decision
 * rather than an accident: every occurrence of the document title sits inside the five files this
 * agent owns, so a retitle is one owner changing one carve-out, and the h1 below is its authority.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import { disclaimers } from '../lib/copy/disclaimers.ts'
import { results } from '../lib/copy/results.ts'
/**
 * Shared chrome, imported rather than transcribed (docs/VOICE.md §12.1; copy review finding F-13).
 * The related-policies heading and the cross-link labels under it appear on more than one of the
 * five policy pages, which makes them chrome rather than policy, and chrome has one owner. The
 * one-line description after each link stays in this file: it says why that policy matters to this
 * page, which is a judgment about this document.
 */
import { nav } from '../lib/copy/nav.ts'
import { pages } from '../lib/copy/pages.ts'

const title = 'Terms of use — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'DelayPilot is an informational tool. It never files a claim, never books or purchases anything, and never represents you. The terms, stated plainly.'

const intro =
  'DelayPilot tells you what is known about a flight, how much to trust it, and which published rules may apply. It never files a claim, never buys or books anything, and never acts as you or for you.'

/** The reviewed date. BaseLayout renders it; this page does not print a second copy. */
const REVIEWED_AT = '2026-09-12'

/**
 * The quoted text below is imported, never retyped.
 *
 * apps/web/src/lib/copy/disclaimers.ts and results.ts (owner: ux-copy-steward) hold the fixed
 * strings transcribed byte for byte from DIRECTIVE.md, and their test re-reads DIRECTIVE.md and
 * fails on a single changed character. Declaring a second copy of a verbatim disclaimer in this
 * file would create exactly the drift that test exists to prevent.
 */
const INDEPENDENCE_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.independence
const FLIGHT_DATA_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.flightData
const PREDICTION_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.prediction
const RIGHTS_DISCLAIMER = disclaimers.rights
const DEMO_LABEL = results.demo
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  kind="policy"
  reviewedAt={REVIEWED_AT}
  nav="terms"
>
  <PageHeader slot="page-header" title="Terms of use" intro={intro} />

  <div class="dpx-article__body">
    <div class="dpx-shell">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>DelayPilot is an information tool, and only that.</strong> It never files a claim, a
          complaint, or a refund request. It never books, purchases, rebooks, or cancels anything. It
          never represents you, and it never signs in anywhere as you. What it does is tell you what is
          known about a flight, how much to trust that information, and which published rules may apply
          to your situation — with the source, the version, and the timestamp attached.
        </p>

        <h2>What DelayPilot is</h2>

        <blockquote>
          <p>{INDEPENDENCE_DISCLAIMER}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Airline, airport, and regulator names, and IATA or ICAO codes, appear on this site only to
          identify a flight, a place, or a rule. Their use does not imply any relationship.
          DelayPilot is not affiliated with, endorsed by, appointed by, or acting for any airline,
          airport, government agency, or regulator, and it does not display any third party's logo,
          wordmark, or brand styling.
        </p>

        <h2>What DelayPilot never does on your behalf</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            It never submits a claim, complaint, refund request, rebooking, or purchase for you —
            not to an airline, not to a regulator, not to anyone.
          </li>
          <li>It never acts as your agent, your representative, or your legal adviser.</li>
          <li>
            It never reads your email inbox, scrapes an airline account, or authenticates as you to
            a third party.
          </li>
          <li>
            It never asks for a booking reference or record locator, a passport, a government ID, a
            Known Traveler Number, a redress number, a card number, or an airline account password.
          </li>
          <li>
            It never classifies an emergency and never gives safety-critical aviation advice.
            Weather and airspace information describes operating conditions, nothing more.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          These are not preferences that a future release might relax. They are invariants the
          codebase is built and reviewed against, and a change to any of them would be a change to
          what this product is.
        </p>

        <h2>Passenger-rights information is an estimate, not a decision</h2>

        <p>
          Where DelayPilot describes passenger rights, it describes what published rules{' '}
          <strong>may</strong> mean for the facts you entered. Every assessment is tied to a named, dated
          rule-set version with links to the official source, and is limited to five outcomes: a rule
          likely applies, may apply, is not indicated, cannot be determined from what is known, or is
          a future rule that is not yet in force. DelayPilot never tells you that a payment is due to
          you, never states that an airline is required to pay you, and never presents an assessment as
          a determination. Only the airline or the relevant regulator can decide your case.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>{RIGHTS_DISCLAIMER}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          <strong>Today, no rule set is in force.</strong> The rights engine carries no rule data, so
          this site produces no rights assessment about a real flight at all. Any passenger-rights writing
          you find here is general explanation with its official source named, and the only rights output
          that appears before a rule set is published is inside clearly labelled demonstration content
          built from an invented itinerary.
        </p>

        <h2>Flight information changes, and can be wrong</h2>

        <p>
          Operational flight information comes from third-party data providers and from official
          aviation sources. It can be incomplete, delayed, or wrong, and it changes while you are
          reading it. Every value DelayPilot displays carries a label saying where it came from and
          how fresh it is, and where no trustworthy answer exists the interface says so rather than
          estimating one.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>{FLIGHT_DATA_DISCLAIMER}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Today no flight-data provider is connected and no provider licence is recorded, so live
          flight data is <strong>Unavailable</strong> and the site says exactly that.
        </p>

        <h2>Risk and connection assessments are estimates</h2>

        <p>
          Delay risk, cancellation risk, and connection assessments are estimates derived from the
          information available at the time. DelayPilot shows a qualitative risk band rather than a
          percentage unless a calibrated model has been validated and deployed, because a number
          implies a precision that an uncalibrated model does not have.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>{PREDICTION_DISCLAIMER}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Demonstration content is fiction</h2>

        <p>
          To show what the product does without paid data, DelayPilot uses a deterministic, invented
          itinerary. Every panel built from it carries this label:
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>{DEMO_LABEL}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Demonstration content never uses a real flight number with invented live details, and is
          never indexed as though it were a real flight.
        </p>

        <h2>No warranty, and no guarantee of availability</h2>

        <p>
          This site is provided as it is, without warranty of accuracy, completeness, or fitness for
          a particular purpose. It may be changed, interrupted, or withdrawn at any time. Do not
          rely on it alone for a decision that cannot be reversed: confirm critical details with the
          operating airline and the airport before you act.
        </p>

        <h2>Using this site</h2>

        <p>
          The site is free to read and needs no account. You may read, print, and share its pages.
          The text, design, and original explanation remain DelayPilot's; regulatory text and
          official source material belong to the bodies that publish it, and is linked rather than
          reproduced wholesale. Do not use this site in a way that breaks the law or that interferes
          with its operation for other readers.
        </p>

        <h2>Liability and governing law</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>This section is deliberately incomplete.</strong> The operator has not yet designated
          a governing jurisdiction, and no limitation of liability has been through legal review. Rather
          than copy a clause from elsewhere and present it as agreed, DelayPilot states the position plainly:
          no such clause is asserted here, and nothing on this page is intended to remove a consumer protection
          that the law of your country gives you. This section will be written once a jurisdiction is
          designated and a lawyer has reviewed the result.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>

        <p>
          These terms are rewritten rather than added to when the product changes — an account
          system, a purchase, a saved trip, or a published rule set each change what there is to
          govern. The reviewed date at the top of this page records when its description last
          matched the site.
        </p>

        <h2>{pages.policy.relatedHeading}</h2>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="/privacy/">{nav.footer.links['/privacy/']}</a> — what is collected today, and what
            never will be.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/editorial-policy/">{nav.footer.links['/editorial-policy/']}</a> — how guidance is
            sourced, reviewed, and corrected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/advertising-policy/">{nav.footer.links['/advertising-policy/']}</a> and{' '}
            <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">{nav.footer.links['/affiliate-disclosure/']}</a> — how DelayPilot
            does and does not make money.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/methodology/">{nav.footer.links['/methodology/']}</a> — the rules above, shown running
            rather than described.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
